# The driver registry: which driver handles which kind of device, as *data*
# (specs/services.md). Adding a driver is adding a row here and a binary to the
# image -- not a code change -- and the day the same file comes from a Devs:
# volume instead of the initrd, only its source changes.
#
# One row per driver, key=value fields:
#   compatible  the device tree's name for the transport
#   id          the virtio device id the registers must answer (0: the
#               compatible string alone decides)
#   prefix,bus  instance names are prefix.busN: blk + virtio -> blk.virtio0
#   binary      the initrd entry the driver starts from
#   memory      the driver's virtqueue memory, in bits (2^n bytes) -- the
#               queue takes the first 8 KiB, the clamp table grows past it
#   window      the shared window its port serves through, in bits; 0 is no
#               window -- the port's answers ride in the envelope itself
#               (aegir/entropy.h)
compatible=virtio,mmio id=2 prefix=blk bus=virtio binary=aegir-virtio-blk memory=14 window=16
compatible=virtio,mmio id=4 prefix=rng bus=virtio binary=aegir-virtio-rng memory=14 window=0
compatible=virtio,mmio id=18 prefix=kbd bus=virtio binary=aegir-virtio-input memory=15 window=0
# The display. One row serves every head: two transports on QEMU's command
# line are two bindings of this row and one aegir-virtio-gpu process each
# (gpu.virtio0, gpu.virtio1 -- specs/services.md). The window is the
# framebuffer itself -- 32 MiB of mega pages, because 3840x2160 at 32 bits a
# pixel fits in 32 MiB -- and a window of 21 bits or more is carved as
# seL4_RISCV_Mega_Page frames, not thousands of 4 KiB caps.
compatible=virtio,mmio id=16 prefix=gpu bus=virtio binary=aegir-virtio-gpu memory=13 window=25
